# Design: TestTimeoutRetries load-sensitivity remedy (T080)

## Problem
The recovery sync in TestTimeoutRetries inherited the fixture's
300 ms RequestTimeout, which exists only so the injected stalled
acknowledgment fails fast. One exchange past 300 ms under a
concurrent make memory-e2e (real CLI processes) failed a full
make check at 29b7774; isolated runs pass in 0.4 s.

## Approach
Two-part remedy, both levers named by the task: the recovery sync
runs on a fresh uploader with the package-standard 10 s
RequestTimeout (all other tests in the package already use it and
held in the failing run), and retries ErrDisconnected for up to
20 s, matching the production Run loop. The 300 ms fault timeout
and every assertion are unchanged.

## Alternatives Considered
- Widen the 300 ms for the whole test — rejected: phase 1 would
  wait the full timeout every run, slowing every check.
- 10 s budget alone — rejected by measurement: under six CPU
  spinners plus two continuous 256 MiB dsync writers, single
  exchanges stall past 10 s (2 of 5 runs failed).
- Serialize the package or calibrate make check to saturated-disk
  load — rejected: the same saturation multiplies the cmd/ohya
  child-process suite past the 20 m per-binary go test timeout,
  so that load is outside the suite's survivable range.

## Verification
Reproducing load: pre-fix fails reliably at the recorded call;
final remedy 10/10 plus whole-package runs. Full parallel
go test ./... under an incident-comparable sustained load
(1.7-2.6x): 19 packages, exit 0. Unloaded make check green.

